}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/2}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319096 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319097 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\cdot {3}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\cdot {3}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319100 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319101 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{32}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{2}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{32}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{2}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] [:db/cas 17592186319103 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319105 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319108 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319108 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+3\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+3)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-(4{\\pi }^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(4{\\pi }^{2}+3-8)-(-3+8) \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-5-5 \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+3\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+3)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-(4{\\pi }^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(4{\\pi }^{2}+3-8)-(-3+8) \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-5-5 \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-10\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319109 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral: