ral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319128 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319133 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319137 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319144 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319145 :question/problem "What is the value of $f(t)=\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}+4}\\,i+{3}^{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,k$ at $t=2?$\n\n$f(2)=$\n{{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-3}} $k$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}+4}\\,i+{3}^{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,k\n$$ at $t=2?$\n\n$f(2)=$\n{{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-3}} $k$"] [:db/cas 17592186319152 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319155 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319156 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319158 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Use the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319159 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319162 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319173 :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}}\n$$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319173 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{3}^{8}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{9}^{4}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{k-4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{k-4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{k-4}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{k-4} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{k-4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{k-4}=1$ has the solution $k=4.$ Now, since ${9}^{k-4}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{k-4}<1$ must be $k<4.$" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{3}^{8}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{9}^{4}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{k-4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{k-4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{k-4}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{k-4} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{k-4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{k-4}=1$ has the solution $k=4.$ Now, since ${9}^{k-4}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{k-4}<1$ must be $k<4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319174 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319174 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319176 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 8{x}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{7}{8}.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 8{x}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319178 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+3x-2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+3x-2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319184 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{5n-2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is divergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{5n-2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\n$$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}\n$$ is divergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}\n$$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319189 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319192 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319198 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\