n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}={z}^{2}\n$$ and the $xz$-plane are two lines, what is the intersection point of these lines?"] [:db/cas 17592186321024 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{6(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{6(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321025 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321026 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321027 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321027 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x. \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x. \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321028 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321028 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321029 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\pi /2}({cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\pi /2}({cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321030 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321030 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(3-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(3\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(3-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(3\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321031 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321035 :question/problem "If \n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5 & -7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 12 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -8 & -10 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\text{rank}(H).$" "If \n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5 & -7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 12 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -8 & -10 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\text{rank}(H).$"] [:db/cas 17592186321036 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321037 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}[4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+3{x}^{2}+4{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)]\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}[4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+3{x}^{2}+4{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)]\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321038 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321039 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{1+{cot}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{1+{cot}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321040 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{3}\\,x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{3}\\,x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321041 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321042 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321044 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321044 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$  and  $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$  and  $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321045 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321045 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  and  $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  and  $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321046 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321046 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-sin\\,(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u