refore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡5x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=5x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=5x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321048 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321048 :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321050 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},\n$$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321052 :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186321058 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321061 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321062 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321062 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=1.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321063 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}.$" "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321065 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321065 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321067 :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7eebd794b43da16658750db0df3a4d4c4b894ddb80f84795ea483aa4d579f56.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/f7/f7eebd794b43da16658750db0df3a4d4c4b894ddb80f84795ea483aa4d579f56.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186321075 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+4}{n+5}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}$ is convergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\l